I picked **candidate 3**. I estimated this in my head and did not simulate it, so the margin over candidates 1 and 2 is thin.

**How the candidates differ.** Six of the plans order every day. Each one orders roughly a fixed multiple of the forecast demand for its arrival day:
- candidate 0 orders about 0.95×;
- candidate 7 about 1.05×;
- candidate 3 about 1.10× for both products;
- candidate 1 about 1.15× for product 0 and 1.20× for product 1;
- candidate 5 about 1.20× for product 0 and 1.10× for product 1;
- candidate 4 about 1.25×.

Candidate 2 orders every 2 days and candidate 6 every 3 days, in larger batches.

**Shortage costs more than surplus.** A missing unit of product 0 loses 8 of margin plus an 8 penalty, so 16 in total. For product 1 the same loss is 7 + 7 = 14. A surplus unit is not lost straight away: it carries over to the next day and costs only storage (0.9 or 0.7 per day) until it spoils after 5 or 4 days. Also, average actual demand is about 1.046× the forecast. Together these favour ordering somewhat above the forecast.

**Over-ordering builds up.** Each daily plan orders the same multiple every day, so the surplus accumulates. At 1.20–1.25× the excess is about 6–8 units of product 0 per day. That stock either pays rising storage or eventually spoils, costing 12 per unit. Candidates 1, 5 and 4 lose money this way. Candidates 0 and 7 order too little and pay frequent shortage penalties. Around 1.10× is the best balance, which is candidate 3.

**Batched plans.**
- Candidate 2 places about 13 fewer orders, saving about 660 in the 51-per-order fee. Each batch sits in storage about one extra day, costing about 57 per 2-day cycle, so about 680 over the month. The two roughly cancel out, and with larger, less frequent deliveries it reacts more slowly to random swings in demand. It is close to candidate 3 but not clearly better.
- Candidate 6 brings in about 250 units at once on top of existing stock, close to the 276-unit warehouse limit. Holding and overflow risk are higher, so it is worse.

**Early days.** Every plan runs short of product 0 on days 0–4, because the first order only arrives on day 5. That loss is the same for all candidates and does not change the ranking.
